{% comment %}
  A working example, self-contained: paste the whole file into the
  plugin's markup. A private plugin has no loader for {% raw %}{% include %}{% endraw %}, so the
  top half is trmnl/selection.liquid verbatim -- keep them in step if you
  edit one. The layout below it is yours to change.
{% endcomment %}

{% comment %}
  Selection logic only -- no layout, no styling. Drop this at the top of
  your markup and everything below it can use `pick` and `card_image`.

  It resolves three settings -- Orientation, Region, Era -- against
  today.json:

    several chosen  -> a card matching one value from each axis the
                       reader set, rotating a day at a time over the
                       combinations that exist
    some chosen     -> the axes they left alone are not constrained
    none chosen     -> today's card from the whole catalogue

  That last line is the rule the whole thing is built around: an
  untouched settings panel means the full catalogue, never an empty
  screen.

  `day_index` is the day number the schedule turns on, so the rotation
  holds all day and moves on by itself at midnight UTC.

  Two things this is careful about, both found by running it rather than
  reading it: a settings keyname and a feed key of the same name collide
  (hence orientation_options/country_options/era_options in the feed),
  and `split` leaves a trailing empty string on some engines, which is
  why nothing here indexes straight into a split array.
{% endcomment %}

{% assign settings = trmnl.plugin_settings.custom_fields_values %}
{% assign SEP = "__" %}

{% comment %}
  A multi-select arrives as an array, a single choice as a string, and
  an untouched field as nothing at all. Normalise all three to a list.
{% endcomment %}
{% assign chosen_orientation = settings.orientation %}
{% if chosen_orientation == nil %}{% assign chosen_orientation = "" | split: "," %}{% endif %}
{% if chosen_orientation.first == nil %}{% assign chosen_orientation = chosen_orientation | split: "," %}{% endif %}

{% comment %}
  Every one of the three is a multi-select, and every one means "all of
  them" when it is empty. That is the whole rule, and it is the reason
  none of them is a pair of on/off switches: two booleans have a state
  -- both off -- that has to mean something, and whatever it is made to
  mean contradicts what the switches say. An empty multi-select has no
  such state to explain.
{% endcomment %}
{% assign chosen_region = settings.region %}
{% if chosen_region == nil %}{% assign chosen_region = "" | split: "," %}{% endif %}
{% if chosen_region.first == nil %}{% assign chosen_region = chosen_region | split: "," %}{% endif %}

{% assign chosen_era = settings.era %}
{% if chosen_era == nil %}{% assign chosen_era = "" | split: "," %}{% endif %}
{% if chosen_era.first == nil %}{% assign chosen_era = chosen_era | split: "," %}{% endif %}

{% comment %}
  A boolean field arrives as the STRING "true" or "false", so it has to
  be compared, not just tested -- {% raw %}{% if show_caption %}{% endraw %} is true even when the
  reader turned it off. An untouched field arrives as nothing, so the
  default matters: the caption is on unless switched off, the credit off
  unless switched on. A fresh install shows a captioned card.
{% endcomment %}
{% assign want_caption = true %}
{% if settings.show_caption == "false" %}{% assign want_caption = false %}{% endif %}

{% assign want_place = true %}
{% if settings.show_place == "false" %}{% assign want_place = false %}{% endif %}

{% assign want_credit = false %}
{% if settings.show_credit == "true" %}{% assign want_credit = true %}{% endif %}

{% comment %}
  What the reader saw -> the keys this feed uses. keys_by_label carries
  every spelling a setting might come back as, because TRMNL sends a
  value derived from the label rather than the label itself.

  Each axis ends up as a comma-terminated string. An axis nobody set
  becomes "all", which constrains nothing -- that is what makes an
  untouched panel mean the whole catalogue.
{% endcomment %}
{% assign o_keys = "" %}
{% for label in chosen_orientation %}
  {% assign lbl = label | strip %}
  {% assign k = keys_by_label[lbl] %}
  {% if k %}{% assign o_keys = o_keys | append: k | append: "," %}{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% if o_keys == "" %}{% assign o_keys = "all," %}{% endif %}

{% assign r_keys = "" %}
{% for label in chosen_region %}
  {% assign lbl = label | strip %}
  {% assign k = keys_by_label[lbl] %}
  {% if k %}{% assign r_keys = r_keys | append: k | append: "," %}{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% if r_keys == "" %}{% assign r_keys = "all," %}{% endif %}

{% assign e_keys = "" %}
{% for label in chosen_era %}
  {% assign lbl = label | strip %}
  {% assign k = keys_by_label[lbl] %}
  {% if k %}{% assign e_keys = e_keys | append: k | append: "," %}{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% if e_keys == "" %}{% assign e_keys = "all," %}{% endif %}

{% assign o_list = o_keys | split: "," %}
{% assign r_list = r_keys | split: "," %}
{% assign e_list = e_keys | split: "," %}

{% comment %} Every combination the reader asked for that the feed actually carries. {% endcomment %}
{% assign wanted = "" %}
{% for o in o_list %}{% if o != "" %}
  {% for c in r_list %}{% if c != "" %}
    {% for e in e_list %}{% if e != "" %}
      {% capture cell %}{{ o }}{{ SEP }}{{ c }}{{ SEP }}{{ e }}{% endcapture %}
      {% if cell_keys contains cell %}
        {% assign wanted = wanted | append: cell | append: "," %}
      {% endif %}
    {% endif %}{% endfor %}
  {% endif %}{% endfor %}
{% endif %}{% endfor %}

{% comment %}
  Nothing matched, so loosen one axis at a time rather than dropping
  straight to the whole catalogue. Someone asking for
  portrait cards from Africa before 1900 has asked for something thin on
  the ground, and should still get a card from Africa.
{% endcomment %}
{% assign n = 0 %}
{% assign wanted_list = wanted | split: "," %}
{% for k in wanted_list %}{% if k != "" %}{% assign n = n | plus: 1 %}{% endif %}{% endfor %}

{% if n == 0 %}
  {% for o in o_list %}{% if o != "" %}
    {% for c in r_list %}{% if c != "" %}
      {% capture cell %}{{ o }}{{ SEP }}{{ c }}{{ SEP }}all{% endcapture %}
      {% if cell_keys contains cell %}{% assign wanted = wanted | append: cell | append: "," %}{% endif %}
    {% endif %}{% endfor %}
  {% endif %}{% endfor %}
  {% assign wanted_list = wanted | split: "," %}
  {% for k in wanted_list %}{% if k != "" %}{% assign n = n | plus: 1 %}{% endif %}{% endfor %}
{% endif %}

{% if n == 0 %}
  {% for c in r_list %}{% if c != "" %}
    {% capture cell %}all{{ SEP }}{{ c }}{{ SEP }}all{% endcapture %}
    {% if cell_keys contains cell %}{% assign wanted = wanted | append: cell | append: "," %}{% endif %}
  {% endif %}{% endfor %}
  {% assign wanted_list = wanted | split: "," %}
  {% for k in wanted_list %}{% if k != "" %}{% assign n = n | plus: 1 %}{% endif %}{% endfor %}
{% endif %}

{% if n == 0 %}
  {% for e in e_list %}{% if e != "" %}
    {% capture cell %}all{{ SEP }}all{{ SEP }}{{ e }}{% endcapture %}
    {% if cell_keys contains cell %}{% assign wanted = wanted | append: cell | append: "," %}{% endif %}
  {% endif %}{% endfor %}
  {% assign wanted_list = wanted | split: "," %}
  {% for k in wanted_list %}{% if k != "" %}{% assign n = n | plus: 1 %}{% endif %}{% endfor %}
{% endif %}

{% if n == 0 %}
  {% assign wanted_list = default | split: "," %}
  {% assign n = 1 %}
{% endif %}

{% comment %} Today's cell: the nth non-empty entry, n rotating by day. {% endcomment %}
{% assign i = day_index | modulo: n %}
{% assign j = 0 %}
{% assign chosen_key = default %}
{% for k in wanted_list %}
  {% if k != "" %}
    {% if j == i %}{% assign chosen_key = k %}{% endif %}
    {% assign j = j | plus: 1 %}
  {% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% comment %}
  Which day is it *here*? Not in UTC -- here, on this device.

  The feed used to carry one day, chosen against the UTC date, so the
  card changed at the same instant everywhere. In Berlin that is 02:05,
  which reads as a new day. In Los Angeles it is 17:05 the previous
  afternoon; in Auckland it lands at midday and changes the card while
  somebody is looking at it.

  TRMNL hands us everything needed to do better: system.timestamp_utc is
  unix seconds, user.utc_offset is that viewer's offset in seconds, and
  the two added together and divided by 86400 is their own local day
  number -- the same integer daily.py counts in.

  The feed carries yesterday, today and tomorrow, which is all three
  values a device anywhere can land on while one file is live (offsets
  run -12 to +14, so 24 hours of file life spans 50 hours of local
  time). Missing or odd values fall back to the day the feed was built
  for, which is what the old behaviour was.
{% endcomment %}
{% assign local_seconds = trmnl.system.timestamp_utc | plus: trmnl.user.utc_offset %}
{% assign local_day = local_seconds | divided_by: 86400 %}
{% assign day_key = local_day | append: "" %}
{% assign day_picks = days[day_key] %}
{% if day_picks == nil %}{% assign day_picks = days[default_day] %}{% endif %}

{% assign pick = day_picks[chosen_key] %}
{% if pick == nil %}{% assign pick = day_picks[default] %}{% endif %}

{% comment %}
  A pick is a list, not an object -- at 282 of them in one file, field
  names alone would cost about 17KB of the 95KB TRMNL allows. Unpack it
  once here so the layout below stays readable. The order is the feed's
  pick_fields.
{% endcomment %}
{% assign card_image = pick[0] %}
{% assign card_title = pick[1] %}
{% assign card_date = pick[2] %}
{% assign card_place = pick[3] %}
{% assign card_publisher = pick[4] %}
{% assign card_credit = pick[5] %}

{% comment %}
  From here on:
    card_image      the postcard, ready to use at any panel size. It is
                    already sized for the largest panel there is
                    (1872x1404, the X) and smaller screens scale it down
                    for free, and daily.py has already asked the archive
                    for it so it is warm -- do NOT rebuild this URL from
                    trmnl.device, or the first device to look pays for a
                    cold render and shows a blank panel.
    card_title      the card's own caption, trimmed to fit
    card_date       "1907" or "1900-1906"
    card_place      "Athens, Region of Attica, Greece"
    card_publisher  who printed it, when the archive knows
    card_credit     the holding institution and collection
    want_caption    whether to draw the caption block at all
    want_place      whether that block includes the place line
    want_credit     whether to print the credit
{% endcomment %}

{% comment %} ---------- layout ---------- {% endcomment %}

<div class="layout">
  <img class="image image-dither image--contain w--max-full h--max-full"
       src="{{ card_image }}">

  {% comment %}
    The whole caption is one conditional, so switching it off leaves the
    card alone on the screen rather than an empty box on top of it.
  {% endcomment %}
  {% if want_caption %}
    <div class="absolute bottom--2 left--2 z--2 p--2 w--max-[80cqw] lg:w--max-[75cqw] bg--canvas outline outline--strong">
      <div class="flex flex--col gap--xsmall">
        <div class="content">
          <span class="title title--small">{{ card_title }}</span>

          {% if want_place and card_place != "" %}
            <span class="label label--small">{{ card_place }} &middot; {{ card_date }}</span>
          {% else %}
            <span class="label label--small">{{ card_date }}</span>
          {% endif %}

          {% if card_publisher != "" %}
            <span class="label label--small">Printed by {{ card_publisher }}</span>
          {% endif %}

          {% if want_credit %}
            <span class="label label--small">{{ card_credit }}</span>
          {% endif %}
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  {% endif %}
</div>
